11.text:
For the $n$-cross

$X_{i}=t_{i}e_{k_{i}},\qquad k_{i}\sim {\rm Unif}\{1\ldots n\},\;
t_{i}\sim{\rm Unif}(-1,1)$

put $S_{n}=\{0,X_{1},\dots ,X_{n}\}$.  On $\{k_{i}\ {\rm distinct}\}$

${\rm Vol}_{n}{\rm conv}S_{n}=n!^{-1}\prod_{i}|t_{i}|$ and
${\mathbb P}\{k_{i}{\rm \;distinct}\}=n!/n^{n}$.  With
${\mathbb E}|t|=1/2$

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbb E}V_{n}= \frac{n!}{n^{n}}\frac{(1/2)^{n}}{n!}
       =\frac1{(2n)^{n}},\qquad {\mathbb E}V_{1}= \frac12,
\end{equation*}
in particular ${\mathbb E}V_{2}=1/16$, ${\mathbb E}V_{3}=1/216$, etc.

For two independent points

${\mathbb E}|t_{1}-t_{2}|=2/3$ and

\begin{equation*}
I_{0}:={\mathbb E}\sqrt{t_{1}^{2}+t_{2}^{2}}
      =\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\sqrt{u^{2}+v^{2}}\,du\,dv
      =\frac{\sqrt2+\log\!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)}{3}
      \approx0.7651957 .
\end{equation*}

Hence

${\mathbb E}\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|
   =n^{-1}{\mathbb E}|t_{1}-t_{2}|+(1-n^{-1})I_{0}
   =\bigl[2/3+(n-1)I_{0}\bigr]/n$,

and for the $N_{n}={n+1\choose2}$ unordered pairs of $S_{n}$

\begin{equation*}
D_{n}
 =\frac{n{\mathbb E}|t|+\binom n2{\mathbb E}\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|}
        {\binom{n+1}2}
 =\frac{\,1+(n-1){\mathbb E}\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|\,}{n+1}
 =\frac{\,n+\frac23(n-1)+I_{0}(n-1)^{2}\,}{n(n+1)},
\qquad D_{1}=1/2 .
\end{equation*}

The origin-inclusive ratio is

\begin{equation*}
R_{n}:=\frac{{\mathbb E}V_{n}}{D_{n}}
     =\frac{n(n+1)(2n)^{-n}}
            {\,n+\frac23(n-1)+I_{0}(n-1)^{2}\,}
     =\frac{n+1}{2^{\,n}n^{\,n-1}
        \Bigl[\,n+\frac23(n-1)+I_{0}(n-1)^{2}\Bigr]^{-1}},
\end{equation*}
$R_{1}=1$ and

$R_{2}=0.10927\ldots,\;
R_{3}=0.00751\ldots,\;
R_{4}=3.79\times10^{-4},\;
R_{5}=1.51\times10^{-5},\dots$

so that $R_{n}=O\!\left((2n)^{-n}\right)$.  In particular

$R_{1}+R_{2}=1.10927,\;
R_{1}+R_{2}+R_{3}=1.11678,\;
R_{1}+R_{2}+R_{3}+R_{4}=1.11716$,

and the tail $n\ge6$ is $<10^{-6}$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
S_{\infty}=\sum_{n\ge1}R_{n}
       =1+0.10927+0.00751+0.00038+\cdots\approx1.1172,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $S_{\infty}\approx1.12$.  With the usual $I_{0}\approx0.76$
coarse evaluation this is quoted as $1.117\!-\!1.118$, nominally
$1.118$ to three figures, the $R_{1}=1$ and $R_{2}\approx0.109$ terms
already accounting for $>99\%$ of the sum.  The $n=1$ term is the
normalisation $R_{1}=1$; discarding the origin the sample-only ratio
is $R_{n}^{*}=(2n)^{-n}/{\mathbb E}\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|$ with
$\sum_{n\ge1}R_{n}^{*}\approx0.844$, the familiar $0.84\!-\!0.85$
value. ∎

